    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Recent work on numerical modeling of normal faults has demonstrated a number of important consequences for the effect of a fault's 3D geometry on the resultant stress and displacement field. However, existing 3D models mainly address faults that are frictionless, with the exception of cohesive end-zone models that consider a region of increased friction along a fault tipline. A 3D modeling approach is described in which modeled faults are given frictional characteristics dependent upon burial depth. The slip is governed by the Coulomb criterion, with the rock cohesion set at zero. The friction controls the resultant slip distributions and influences the associated stress and displacement fields. Normal faults are expected to slip more towards the upper part, above the fault center. In this region of the fault, the perturbed stress field is also conducive to the growth of joints along the fault tipline.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
